#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 2.6.48
# DESCRIPTION
# Find the derivative of a function using the chain rule

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Chain rule (with trigonometric functions))
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('2.6')
## Problem1('48')
## KEYWORDS('Derivative', 'Chain rule', 'tangent line')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$a = random(2, 8, 1);
$p = random(3, 7, 1);

$p = $p + 1 if 2 * int($a * $p / 2) != $a * $p;

$ap  = $a * $p;
$x0  = Formula("pi/4");
$pm1 = $p - 1;
$y0  = $a;
$m   = Compute("-$ap*2");

$f  = Formula(" $a*cot(x)**$p ")->reduce;
$g  = Formula(" cot(x) ");
$Df = Formula(" -$ap*cot(x)^$pm1*csc^2(x)")->reduce;

$line = Formula("$y0+$m*(x-$x0)")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML

Find the equation of the tangent line to the graph of  [` y(x) =[$f] `] at [`` x = \frac{\pi}{4}``].

Equation of the tangent line is [` y = `] [_]{$line}{50}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Taking [`  u(x) = [$g] `] in the generalized derivative formula for [` u^[$p]`] yields

 [``` \frac{d \;\left[[$f]\right]}{dx}  = \frac{d \;\left([$a] u^[$p]\right)}{du}\frac{du}{dx}   =  [$Df]. ```]

Now, when [`` x = [$x0] ``], [` y =  [$y0] `] and [`` y'([$x0]) =  [$m] ``]

Hence, equation of tangent line is [`` y = [$y0] +[$m](x-[$x0]) ``]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
